<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mrs. Burney is a 59-year-old woman eight days after a right total mastectomy with sentinel node biopsy for multicentric hormone receptor-positive breast cancer. Her surgical drain was removed two days ago, when the surgeon also gave her the pathology: margins clear and the three sentinel nodes free of cancer. Since then she has felt a soft, sloshing swelling grow under the skin flap below the scar, with tightness when she raises her arm. She has no fever, chills, redness, warmth, or drainage from the wound. Pain is 2 out of 10 on scheduled acetaminophen; she finished ibuprofen three days ago and has not needed any opioid. She is doing her shoulder exercises. She came in today rather than wait, asking to know exactly what would be done. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, after drain removal</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Right chest wall below the mastectomy scar</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Soft, fluid-filled swelling with tightness</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild; pain 2 out of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Day 8 after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Drain removed two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Tighter when she raises her arm</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No fever, redness, warmth, drainage, or arm swelling</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

